    id: 5,
    slug: "sewer-backup-what-to-do-ontario",
    title: "Sewer Backup: What to Do Right Now (Ontario Emergency Guide)",
    excerpt: "A sewer backup is a health emergency. Here is exactly what to do in the first hour and how to prevent it from happening again.",
    content: "Discovering a sewer backup in your London, Ontario home is a homeowner's nightmare. It is messy, stressful, and can pose serious health risks. When sewage starts to overflow from your drains, toilets, or basement floor, knowing what to do immediately can minimize damage and protect your family. As a family-owned business serving London and surrounding areas since 1970, Discount Drain understands these emergencies. Here is your immediate action plan.\n\n### Immediate Steps When Your Sewer Backs Up\n\nYour first priority is safety. Sewer water is contaminated and can carry harmful bacteria and viruses. If the backup is extensive and water is near electrical outlets or appliances, it is best to turn off your home's main electrical power supply if you can do so safely. If not, stay clear of any standing water.\n\nNext, stop all water use in your home. This means no flushing toilets, no running taps, no showering, and no using dishwashers or washing machines. Every drop of water you use will only add to the backup. Locate your main water shut-off valve, usually in your basement or utility room, and turn it off. This will prevent any more clean water from entering your plumbing system and potentially worsening the situation.\n\nTry to contain the spill as much as possible. Use old towels, rags, or sandbags to create barriers around the affected area. If you have a wet/dry vacuum, you can begin to remove standing water, but always wear protective gear like rubber gloves and boots.\n\n### Understanding the Health Hazards\n\nSewer water, also known as blackwater, contains a cocktail of pathogens, including E. coli, Salmonella, and various viruses. Direct contact can lead to skin infections, gastrointestinal illnesses, and respiratory problems. Beyond immediate illness, standing sewage can quickly lead to mold growth, which presents its own set of health concerns, especially for those with allergies or respiratory conditions. Always wear appropriate personal protective equipment, including heavy-duty rubber gloves, waterproof boots, and a mask, when dealing with sewage.\n\n### What NOT to Do\n\nIn a panic, it is easy to make mistakes that can worsen the problem or put you at risk. First, do not use harsh chemical drain cleaners. These products are often ineffective against severe blockages and can damage your pipes, making repairs more difficult and costly. They also add dangerous fumes to your home and can be harmful if mixed with sewage.\n\nDo not ignore the problem, hoping it will go away. A sewer backup is a serious issue that requires prompt attention. Delaying action can lead to more extensive damage, higher repair costs, and prolonged health risks. Finally, unless you are a trained professional, do not attempt complex repairs yourself. You could inadvertently cause more damage or expose yourself to unnecessary hazards.\n\n### When to Call Emergency Services\n\nIf you are experiencing a sewer backup, your first call should be to a professional drain and sewer company like Discount Drain. We offer 24/7 emergency services because we know these problems do not wait for business hours. Our experienced technicians can quickly diagnose the cause of the backup, whether it is a clog, tree roots, or a damaged pipe, and provide effective solutions. We have specialized equipment, like our free camera inspection service, to pinpoint the exact issue without guesswork.\n\nIf the backup affects multiple fixtures throughout your home, or if your neighbours are experiencing similar issues, it might indicate a problem with the municipal sewer main. In this case, contact the City of London's public works department. They are responsible for the main sewer lines.\n\n### Preventing Future Sewer Backups\n\nPrevention is always better than cure. Be mindful of what goes down your drains. Grease, cooking oils, and food scraps should never be poured down the sink. \"Flushable\" wipes, feminine hygiene products, and paper towels should also go in the trash, not the toilet. These items do not break down in water and are major contributors to blockages.\n\nTree roots are a common culprit for sewer line damage, especially in older homes. Consider planting trees away from your sewer lines. Regular maintenance, including professional drain cleaning and our free camera inspection, can identify potential root intrusion early. Installing a backwater valve, which is now often required by the Ontario Building Code for new construction or major renovations in certain areas, can prevent sewage from flowing back into your home during municipal sewer surcharges.\n\n### Ontario Insurance Considerations\n\nStandard homeowner's insurance policies typically do not cover sewer backups. You usually need to add specific \"sewer backup\" or \"water backup and sump pump overflow\" coverage to your policy. It is vital to review your policy with your insurance provider to understand your coverage limits and deductibles. Document everything: take photos and videos of the damage, keep records of all expenses, and note the timeline of events. This documentation will be crucial when filing a claim.\n\n### Why London Homes Built Before 1980 Are More Vulnerable\n\nMany homes in London, Ontario, built before 1980, often have original clay tile sewer pipes. These pipes are brittle, prone to cracking, and have joints that can shift over time. This makes them highly susceptible to tree root intrusion, which seeks out moisture and nutrients, easily penetrating small cracks and loose joints. Once inside, roots grow, creating significant blockages and eventually crushing the pipe.\n\nModern homes use more durable PVC pipes, which are less prone to these issues. If your home has older clay pipes, regular inspections are particularly important. Discount Drain specializes in diagnosing and repairing these older systems, offering solutions like trenchless repair, which can fix damaged pipes without extensive digging, saving your lawn and landscaping.\n\n### Your Local London Experts Are Here to Help\n\nA sewer backup is a serious issue, but you do not have to face it alone. Discount Drain has been a trusted, family-owned name in London, Ontario, for drain and sewer services since 1970. We pride ourselves on honest work, fair pricing, and lasting solutions, backed by our 20-year warranty on many services. From emergency service to preventative maintenance and trenchless repairs, we are here to protect your home.\n\nDo not let a sewer backup ruin your day. Call Discount Drain right now for immediate assistance or to schedule a free camera inspection. We are available 24/7 to help London homeowners.\n\nCall us today: 519-451-8342",
