    title: "Why You Need a Sewer Inspection Before Buying a Home in London Ontario",
    excerpt: "A standard home inspection does not cover the sewer line. Here is why a pre-purchase sewer camera inspection is one of the smartest things a London Ontario home buyer can do.",
    content: "Buying a home in London Ontario is one of the largest financial decisions most people make. A standard home inspection covers the visible and accessible components of the property: the roof, the electrical panel, the furnace, the windows. What it does not cover is the sewer line running from the house to the city main underground.\n\nFor many London Ontario homes, particularly those built before 1980, the sewer line is one of the most significant deferred maintenance items on the property. And because it is underground, it is invisible until it fails.\n\n## What a Standard Home Inspection Misses\n\nA licensed home inspector in Ontario is not required to inspect the sewer line as part of a standard inspection. Most inspectors will note the age of the home and flag that older homes may have clay tile sewer lines, but they cannot tell you the condition of the pipe without a camera inspection.\n\nClay tile sewer lines were the standard in London Ontario until the 1960s and 1970s when plastic pipe became common. Clay tile has a lifespan of 50 to 100 years under good conditions, but root intrusion, soil movement, and ground settling can cause failures much earlier. A home built in 1955 with an original clay tile sewer line may have a pipe that is functioning adequately today but is one heavy rainstorm or root intrusion event away from a backup.\n\n## What a Camera Inspection Reveals\n\nA sewer camera inspection pushes a high-resolution camera through the drain line from a cleanout access point, recording footage of the full length of the accessible pipe. The inspection can identify:\n\nRoot intrusion at clay tile joints, ranging from fine root hairs to full root masses that are partially or completely blocking the pipe.\n\nCracks, fractures, or offset joints where sections of pipe have shifted relative to each other.\n\nCorrosion or buildup inside cast iron pipes that reduces flow capacity.\n\nCollapsed sections where the pipe has lost its structural integrity.\n\nThe location of the connection to the city main, which helps determine whether any problems are on the private side (the homeowner's responsibility) or the city side.\n\n## How Much Does a Pre-Purchase Inspection Cost\n\nAt Discount Drain, the camera inspection is free when combined with a service call. For a standalone pre-purchase inspection, the cost is typically $150 to $300 depending on the length and accessibility of the line. This is a small investment relative to the cost of a sewer line repair or replacement, which can range from $3,500 for a trenchless repair to $15,000 or more for full excavation and replacement.\n\n## What to Do If Problems Are Found\n\nIf the camera inspection reveals root intrusion, cracks, or other issues, you have several options. You can negotiate a price reduction with the seller to account for the cost of repair. You can ask the seller to repair the line before closing. Or you can proceed with the purchase knowing what you are taking on and budget accordingly.\n\nIn some cases, the problems found are minor and can be managed with regular drain cleaning. In other cases, the inspection reveals a sewer line that needs immediate attention. Either way, you are making an informed decision rather than discovering the problem after you have moved in.\n\n## Booking a Pre-Purchase Inspection\n\nDiscount Drain performs pre-purchase sewer inspections throughout London Ontario and Southwestern Ontario. We can typically schedule an inspection within one to two business days, which fits within most real estate transaction timelines.\n\nCall 519-451-8342 or request an appointment online. We will provide a written report of our findings that you can use in your purchase negotiations.",
    metaTitle: "Pre-Purchase Sewer Inspection London Ontario | Discount Drain",
    metaDesc: "A standard home inspection does not include a sewer camera inspection. For older London homes, this is a significant gap that can cost buyers thousands after closing.",
